WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:08.026
Environmental justice includes reducing harmful exposure and increasing access to conditions that support health.

2
00:00:08.026 --> 00:00:15.708
Public facilities, physical activity, healthy food, safe housing, mobility, parks, and civic participation form a connected system.

3
00:00:15.708 --> 00:00:25.912
This independent lesson draws from California DOJ's Best Practices for Implementing SB one thousand and current Government Code section sixty-five thousand three hundred two.

4
00:00:25.912 --> 00:00:27.964
Verify current law and local facts.

5
00:00:27.964 --> 00:00:29.430
This is not legal advice.

6
00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:30.849
Presence is not access.

7
00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:34.488
A park can be nearby but separated by a dangerous crossing.

8
00:00:34.488 --> 00:00:40.286
A facility can exist but lack accessible hours, language support, shade, or disability access.

9
00:00:40.286 --> 00:00:43.000
A food outlet can be close but unaffordable.

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.701
Evaluate the complete path between a person and the benefit.

11
00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:52.190
Use five access dimensions: proximity, affordability, safety, usability, and reliability.

12
00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.151
Add quality and capacity where evidence permits.

13
00:00:55.151 --> 00:00:57.680
Measure different times and travel modes.

14
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.874
Identify who maintains the facility and whether operation is funded.

15
00:01:01.874 --> 00:01:03.735
Avoid a single citywide average.

16
00:01:03.735 --> 00:01:08.445
Compare neighborhoods and populations at a useful scale while protecting privacy.

17
00:01:08.445 --> 00:01:12.633
Combine network analysis with direct observation and community accounts.

18
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:18.099
If a dataset counts an asset but residents report it is unusable, investigate the discrepancy.

19
00:01:18.099 --> 00:01:19.262
Coordinate policies.

20
00:01:19.262 --> 00:01:21.763
Land use can reserve sites and permit uses.

21
00:01:21.763 --> 00:01:23.682
Capital budgets build facilities.

22
00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:25.659
Operations budgets keep them open.

23
00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:27.462
Transportation connects people.

24
00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:31.649
Housing policy affects whether intended beneficiaries can remain nearby.

25
00:01:31.649 --> 00:01:33.526
Torrance application segment.

26
00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:40.773
This original exercise cites the City of Torrance General Plan page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

27
00:01:40.773 --> 00:01:45.497
Choose one hypothetical public facility or health-supporting destination.

28
00:01:45.497 --> 00:01:51.385
Map a realistic walking, rolling, transit, and driving trip from several residential areas.

29
00:01:51.385 --> 00:01:55.721
Audit crossings, shade, seating, schedule, accessibility, and cost.

30
00:01:55.721 --> 00:01:57.662
Ask users what the map misses.

31
00:01:57.662 --> 00:02:04.715
Develop one land-use action, one capital action, and one operational action, each with an agency and measure.

32
00:02:04.715 --> 00:02:10.992
Do not claim the exercise describes existing inequity or determines legal compliance in Torrance.

33
00:02:10.992 --> 00:02:12.199
Access is a chain.

34
00:02:12.199 --> 00:02:23.063
An equitable plan must connect location, transportation, affordability, design, operation, and housing stability so that an apparent benefit becomes a usable one.

35
00:02:23.063 --> 00:02:27.489
The DOJ source text used is state-authored public-domain material.

36
00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:33.323
Third-party appendices, datasets, maps, graphics, and linked publications are excluded.

37
00:02:33.323 --> 00:02:35.067
No endorsement is implied.
